WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:07.859
SIERRA: When we hear about Russia, and we
hear about it pretty often, the news usually

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:09.160
isn't good.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.920
If you were to Google the country on the day
this episode was released, for example, you'd

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.270
see a story on the jailing of political dissident
Alexei Navalny and a new deployment of Russian

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.130
forces in Syria's civil war.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.810
At this point, we're used to hearing this
kind of stuff when it comes to Russia.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:30.199
Hacking, military muscle, support for authoritarian
regimes the whole world over.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:33.210
But what's the story behind that reputation?

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.980
Their power isn't derived from their economy
- Russia's GDP is smaller than Italy's, for

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:38.980
example.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.300
So how is it able to exert such a steady influence
in global events?

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.430
And why does it pursue the strategies that
it does?

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.640
I'm Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.430
Today, Russia.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:58.890
CNN: For the eighth time, Russia has vetoed
a proposed UN Security Council resolution

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.760
regarding the horrors and atrocities going
on in Syria over this six-year plus war.”

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:03.760
https://youtu.be/Ae80m8Efk7M?t=89

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.840
Indus News: And other news, Russia has reportedly
concluded the delivery of a second S-400 missile

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.840
system to China.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.110
Bloomberg: It turns out more US agencies were
targeted by the suspected Russian hack.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.320
The Department of Homeland Security was hit,
and the New York Times says, so are parts

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.320
of the Defense Department.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.320
Barack Obama: Russians intended to meddle,
and they meddled.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.670
Mitt Romney: I think that President Putin
represents a real threat to the stability

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:24.720
and peace of the world.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:33.970
SIERRA: So, Russia is one of those countries
that you hear about a lot in the news, even

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.670
if you don't pay much attention to international
relations, right?

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:43.090
So is it really as powerful and influential
a country as it seems to be?

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.649
I mean, how powerful is Russia?

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:49.710
STENT: Well, it depends on how you want to
measure power.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.259
My name is Angela Stent.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:57.530
I am the director of the Center for Eurasian,
Russian, and East European studies at Georgetown

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:58.630
University.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.189
It is the only other nuclear superpower.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:07.799
The United States and Russia between themselves
possess 90% of the world's nuclear weapons.

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:12.489
So we have to listen to it, we have to pay
attention to it because it can destroy us

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:14.660
many times over.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.110
It has a seat on the United Nations Security
Council.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.489
It inherited that when the Soviet Union collapsed,
the Soviet Union had a seat, Russia now has

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:28.630
that seat, a permanent seat, and that means
it has a veto.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:34.940
So it can make it very difficult for the United
States and its allies to carry out certain

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:36.050
policies.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.860
And we see that all the time.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.159
It is an energy superpower.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:46.060
It has the world's possibly largest reserves
of oil and gas; Europe depends a lot on the

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.700
energy they produce.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.010
And increasingly, the Chinese are buying their
energy.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:56.190
DOUGHERTY: If you look at their nuclear weapons,
which is really number one, they can destroy

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.080
the United States and probably most of the
world.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.720
So yes, they are powerful.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.940
There's no question.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.980
SIERRA: This is Jill Dougherty.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.299
She served as Moscow Bureau chief during a
three-decade career at CNN.

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:15.390
She currently teaches at Georgetown University
and is a global fellow at the Woodrow Wilson

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.099
Center in Washington, DC.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:21.209
DOUGHERTY: Now, when you go beyond that, there
are many ways that you could count it.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:27.080
But I would say conventional military; they're
also quite powerful.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:33.489
If they went into a conflict with NATO, the
United States and NATO together, they would

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:36.300
probably lose, but they are very powerful.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.640
They are developing new weapons.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.959
And they have a sophisticated, educated populace,
and they put as much money as possible into

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:46.349
their military.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:53.379
SIERRA: Russia is not afraid to use its military
prowess - whether it's in Syria or Ukraine

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:56.420
- and don't worry, we'll get to both of those
later.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:01.030
But their military investments are also a
significant source of profit.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:05.629
Russia is the world's second-largest arms
exporter next to the United States.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:12.829
Its most famous product is the AK 47, designed
in 1949 and still used regularly in conflicts

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:14.189
all over the world.

00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:20.820
But Russia also makes highly sophisticated
weapons, like the S-400 anti-aircraft system,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.550
which poses a threat to the most advanced
jets used by the United States and its allies.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:31.840
Russia's military capability and its enormous
nuclear arsenal form the backbone of its unique

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.630
position in the world.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.870
But it's not what we hear about most often
in the news.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:43.360
SESTANOVICH: There is every reason to think
of Russia as important and powerful.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.980
But the reasons often change.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:46.780
I'm Steve Sestanovich.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.660
I'm a senior fellow at the Council of Foreign
Relations, working on Russia and Eurasian

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:51.660
Affairs.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.850
I also teach at Columbia University.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:05:01.000
I think there are new reasons that people
mention often for why Russia matters.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.160
And in fact, some of the most important ones
that you hear most often these days would

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.740
not have been mentioned by anybody ten years
ago.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:20.130
You'd hear a lot today about Russia's cyber
capabilities, the fact that it can use those

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:26.070
to put American infrastructure at risk or
interfere in elections.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:32.800
You might also hear that Russia has become
an agitator for populist nationalism, that

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:41.250
it has kind of stoked a backlash against democratic
institutions and liberal internationalist

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.169
ideas, particularly in the West.

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:49.110
SIERRA: These are the aspects of Russia we
hear about most often in the news - an ability

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:54.979
to infiltrate and exert influence all over
the world, whether through espionage, hacking,

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:57.190
or disinformation campaigns.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.720
In short, Russia isn't afraid to get its hands
dirty.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.380
Other countries do these things too, but Russia
seems to have a unique willingness to use

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.020
every capability at its disposal.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:12.410
And in order to understand this Russia, you
have to first understand something about the

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:14.250
man who runs the place.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:22.349
A man whose name is often used as a stand-in
for the country itself.

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:29.319
DOUGHERTY: He believes Russia is a great power.

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:32.270
It was a great power under the czars.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:37.570
It was a great power under the Soviet Union
when he would argue it has saved the world

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.560
from the Nazis.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:45.530
And he believes that culturally and in every
other way, Russia is a major power, and he

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.050
wants to put it back on the stage.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:53.580
He wants it to be at the table when anything
is being negotiated.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.810
He wants Russia's influence around the world.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:01.879
He's not dictating, kind of like the United
States, "This is the type of democracy you

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:05.819
should follow," but he does believe that Russia
should have influence.

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:11.770
SIERRA: Vladimir Putin was a KGB officer before
the collapse of the Soviet Union.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.910
This event, and Russia's subsequent economic
chaos, is widely seen as a formative moment

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:22.360
in his life, one that clarified his goal of
restoring Russia's former greatness.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.810
Though some saw his rise as unlikely, he became
Russia's president in 1999 and has led the

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.500
country ever since.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:34.590
STENT: As he became president, he published
something called the millennial statement,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:40.009
and this is now the year 2000, essentially
saying that he wanted to rebuild a strong

00:07:40.009 --> 00:07:41.539
state in Russia.

00:07:41.539 --> 00:07:47.070
There'd been terrible economic ravages; really,
there was a crash of the ruble, the currency,

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.070
in 1998.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:51.300
So the country was economically really suffering.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:57.240
And so I think, you know, his vision at that
point came from his training in the KGB, his

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:02.910
patriotism, one could call it nationalism,
but really wanting to build a strong state

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:03.940
with a strong leader.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:09.319
And I think that has informed him since then,
and what's so interesting is he's been in

00:08:09.319 --> 00:08:11.659
power for twenty years now.

00:08:11.659 --> 00:08:15.710
SIERRA: Yeah, I was gonna say you said 2000.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:16.710
It's 2020!

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:17.710
STENT: Right, it's 2020.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.720
So he's seen Western leaders come and go.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:26.310
And he knows their weaknesses, their foibles,
and he's an element of continuity there, so

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.700
that gives him a great advantage.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:34.029
SIERRA: So how has Putin been so successful
at playing what you've described as a weak

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:35.340
hand for Russia?

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.830
STENT: At the age of 24, he became the judo
champion in Leningrad.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.519
Now, why is it important to emphasize the
judo?

00:08:43.519 --> 00:08:50.350
It is because I think the skills that he learned
in judo that even if you are weaker than your

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.100
opponent, you can sense their own distraction.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:59.350
If you're very aware of their own weaknesses,
you can take advantage of them and prevail

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:00.350
over them.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:04.779
So these judo skills, I think, he's used throughout
his life.

00:09:04.779 --> 00:09:10.899
He's been successful because he has been able
to take advantage of opportunities that have

00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:13.170
been presented to him by the West.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.540
I would say, you know, after the collapse
of the Soviet Union, the United States and

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:21.230
its allies thought we were going to live in
a much more liberal world.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:25.450
Communism has gone, and Russia will want to
become like us.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.820
Well, it turned out we got that one wrong.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:32.380
Because I think what we failed to understand
was that Russians are Russians, and they do

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.190
have their own view of the world.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:38.149
They certainly believe they have a unique
civilization; it's different from that of

00:09:38.149 --> 00:09:39.339
the West.

00:09:39.339 --> 00:09:42.140
And they don't want to become like the West.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.570
And so we didn't really have a plan.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:44.730
And Putin had a plan.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:51.490
I mean, even in 2016, if you think about,
when Russia interfered in the 2016 election,

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:55.600
in terms of social media and in terms of cyber,
we were really asleep at the wheel.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:00.700
By the time we woke up to what was happening,
it was pretty late in the game.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.570
And so he knows how to do that.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:04.350
He knows how to do that very well.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:09.680
Sometimes, of course, he makes mistakes, but
he's really perfected the art of playing this

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:15.589
weak hand well, and also of being able to
act as a spoiler on issues where the United

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:17.920
States and its allies have different interests.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.950
SIERRA: If you want to see an example of Putin's
judo skills, you only need to look at Russia's

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:28.510
cyberattacks and digital disinformation campaigns,
which skillfully exploit weaknesses in one

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:31.709
of the West's biggest strengths - its tech
infrastructure.

00:10:31.709 --> 00:10:36.010
DOUGHERTY: Cyber is cheap; this is what we
all know.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:40.829
You can hire people for very little money
with a big bang.

00:10:40.829 --> 00:10:46.970
I mean, the results of all of these operations
are really quite great.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:50.950
And you don't have to spill blood, and you
don't have to spend money on weapons.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:56.260
So Russia is definitely using this as much
as it can.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:02.820
So let's go back to 2016 and what we refer
to as the interference in the American election.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.560
CBSN: Turning out a new report that shows
the massive scope of Russia’s disinformation

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.010
campaign during the 2016 presidential election.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:14.190
PBS Newshour: This is the first glimpse we
got into Russia’s interference in the election.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:19.339
It’s summer of 2016, and hacked and stolen
emails from the Hillary Clinton campaign and

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:22.769
the Democratic National Committee were publicly
released.

00:11:22.769 --> 00:11:28.290
CBSN: Facebook, Google, and Twitter all disclosed
they had found Russian interference on their

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:29.290
sites.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:34.870
DOUGHERTY: So Russia, at that point, was trying
to influence the election.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:40.880
As we know from congressional investigations
and U.S. intelligence agencies, they were

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:46.410
trying to damage Hillary Clinton, who they
believed was going to win the election.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:52.279
By default, they were trying to help Donald
Trump, whom they perceived as a better possibility

00:11:52.279 --> 00:11:57.720
for doing the things that President Putin
wanted, like getting rid of sanctions and

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.320
maybe improving the relationship.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.700
SIERRA: As we found out in an earlier episode,
there's no way to be sure that Russia changed

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.220
the outcome of the 2016 election.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.630
What is certain, according to the consensus
of the U.S. intelligence community, is that

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.980
Russia did interfere on a vast scale.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.209
Russia is very good at online disinformation.

00:12:19.209 --> 00:12:22.290
But it's also good at outright cyberattacks.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.650
News of the most recent attack broke in December.

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:29.529
And while it's still being investigated, it's
already clear that Russia was able to gain

00:12:29.529 --> 00:12:35.480
access to secret information from some of
the U.S. government's most important agencies

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.589
and also from hundreds of corporate and private
entities.

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:42.449
GOOD MORNING AMERICA: Sources are telling
ABC News that this bridge went undetected

00:12:42.449 --> 00:12:46.740
for six months and that among the agencies
targeted were Commerce, Treasury, State, Homeland

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:48.180
Security, and even NIH.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:54.410
GOOD MORNING AMERICA: Lawmakers are now comparing
the hack to everything from the virtual invasion

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.540
to Pearl Harbor.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:03.709
DOUGHERTY: That is a very different operation
that was not to sow confusion or intrude in

00:13:03.709 --> 00:13:04.709
American elections.

00:13:04.709 --> 00:13:10.360
That was really an intelligence gathering
operation, at least as far as we know, in

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.970
order to get information.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:17.880
And the question that we don't know is why
did they want that information?

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.920
What kind of information did they want?

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.570
And what were they planning on doing with
it?

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:27.339
SIERRA: It seems like there are a variety
of techniques, and organizations, and goals

00:13:27.339 --> 00:13:28.350
at work here.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:35.410
I mean, one simple result of all this is that
it has changed my own perception of the internet

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:36.709
and security.

00:13:36.709 --> 00:13:41.570
It's to the point where if Slack goes down
or Google Docs, it can feel very much like,

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:43.890
"Hey, there goes Russia again!"

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.010
How alarmed should we be about Russia's cyber
capabilities?

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:53.320
DOUGHERTY: I think we actually should be quite
alarmed because they are very sophisticated

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:55.079
when it comes to this type of thing.

00:13:55.079 --> 00:14:01.540
They also are not constrained by the laws
that exist in the United States about the

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.330
first amendment and privacy.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:09.150
Certainly, like in Europe, privacy laws are
even stronger.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:16.449
It's been described to me by people who really
know this business that they are much more

00:14:16.449 --> 00:14:22.839
nimble and willing to take chances, to take
it right up to the edge, and maybe even over.

00:14:22.839 --> 00:14:28.690
SIERRA: And going over the edge is something
Russia is willing to do, not just in cyberspace

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.389
but in the real world too.

00:14:30.389 --> 00:14:37.000
In 2014, Russia shocked the world by invading
and annexing the Ukrainian territory of Crimea.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.589
In addition, Russia also began supporting
Ukrainian separatists in the country's southeast

00:14:42.589 --> 00:14:43.589
region.

00:14:43.589 --> 00:14:49.370
The ensuing war has killed at least 13,000
people so far and has been described as the

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:50.899
most significant security

00:14:50.899 --> 00:14:58.470
SKY NEWS: The Crimea is a sparkling jewel
set enticingly in the Black Sea.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:03.160
It’s been coveted by many peoples and nations
for over 2000 years of history.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:10.930
ABC World News: Armored Russian vehicles burst
through the walls of Crimea’s Belbek base

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:14.259
today, firing warning shots and throwing sound
grenades.

00:15:14.259 --> 00:15:18.410
U.S. Department of State: Six years ago, Russia
invaded, occupied, and attempted to annex

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:19.540
Ukraine’s Crimean Peninsula.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:25.089
Today Russia’s occupation of Crimea remains
an affront to the international order.

00:15:25.089 --> 00:15:31.180
SESTANOVICH: One of the reasons Russia matters
is that it is a broken up empire.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:37.990
And the borderlands of Russia, all the territories
that used to be part of the Soviet empire,

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:42.060
now have a kind of undefined relationship
with Russia.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:48.889
There are a lot of countries there that are
nervous about the prospect that Russia will

00:15:48.889 --> 00:15:56.639
try to reassert influence over them, try to,
you know, if not gain overt control, at least

00:15:56.639 --> 00:16:00.339
make them a part of its sphere of influence.

00:16:00.339 --> 00:16:06.680
So the Russian periphery is a kind of source
of conflict of international instability.

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:13.139
And Ukraine is the most important part of
that because it was the most populous of the

00:16:13.139 --> 00:16:18.600
former Soviet states that gained independence
in 1991.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:24.339
And sometimes that tension had burst into
real conflict, as you had when the President

00:16:24.339 --> 00:16:32.580
of Ukraine was overthrown in 2014, and the
Russians responded by seizing Ukrainian territory

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:36.509
and by starting a separatist war in the east
of Ukraine.

00:16:36.509 --> 00:16:42.880
I think this has been the deepest crisis that
the Soviet breakup created, but it's not the

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:43.930
only one.

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:49.670
Russia has tensions with almost all of the
former Soviet states.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:55.509
DOUGHERTY: The reason that it happened was
because Russia wanted it back.

00:16:55.509 --> 00:17:04.459
I think President Putin wanted it as a symbol
of his power to reunite Russia and to get

00:17:04.459 --> 00:17:07.860
the property that had belonged to Russia before.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:13.939
It was an extremely popular move in Russia
by President Putin.

00:17:13.939 --> 00:17:20.520
I mean, I was there at the time, and there
were little ribbons that people were wearing,

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.230
"Yes, Crimea is back."

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:32.650
On my refrigerator, I have a magnet, and it
says in Russian, "Khrushchev gave it up."

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:37.789
Then there's a picture of President Putin
looking like a military guy, and it says,

00:17:37.789 --> 00:17:40.030
"And Putin took it back!"

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:43.280
So this was a very popular political move.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.200
So it accomplished what Putin wanted.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:56.169
But in a broader sense, it was the annexation
of territory, and the West would say illegally,

00:17:56.169 --> 00:17:59.780
the first annexation in modern times.

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:05.150
So it broke the rule about taking over the
territory of another country.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:08.000
And that is why it is such a big deal.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.250
It has set a precedent.

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:17.289
But I think there's a bigger issue here too,
which is that President Putin looks at the

00:18:17.289 --> 00:18:23.860
former Soviet Union, which is a very big area,
as kind of his territory.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:29.660
And Russia is intent on continuing to have
control of that area.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.780
That, I think, is what motivates Putin.

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.320
So when you look at Crimea, you can say, "Well,
maybe it was a one-off."

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:40.640
But look at what's happening right now in
Belarus.

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.020
Would Russia send troops in?

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:49.070
Would Russia want to take it over now that
it's involved in what really is a rebellion?

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:52.850
They haven't so far, but that is a concern.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:56.240
SIERRA: I mean, it must be pretty nerve-wracking
if you live in Eastern Europe?

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.720
DOUGHERTY: It is!

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:04.790
SIERRA: A majority of Crimeans, many of whom
are ethnically Russian, celebrated the annexation.

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:07.350
The situation in Belarus is quite different.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:13.030
In August, a democratic uprising seemed poised
to overthrow the country's dictator.

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:17.640
But support from Russia helped him initiate
a crackdown that has so far kept that from

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:18.640
happening.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:22.970
And Russia's willingness to act daringly to
defend its interests is not limited to its

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:24.860
former Soviet empire.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:29.700
The best example of this is its involvement
in Syria's civil war, which has been described

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:37.600
as the worst humanitarian crisis of our lifetime,
displacing some 11.5 million people and killing

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:40.320
hundreds of thousands more.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:46.659
STENT: So in the Soviet days, the Soviet Union
had a close relationship with Syria; it was

00:19:46.659 --> 00:19:48.590
quite prominent in the Middle East.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.980
After the Soviet collapse, it withdrew.

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:53.210
It didn't have the wherewithal to do that.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:55.620
But the relationship with Syria was still
important.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:20:01.130
There's been a civil war going on in Syria
for a number of years now, really, since around

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:02.600
2011.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:07.890
And the leader of Syria, President Assad,
was someone who was, you know, closely allied

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:08.890
with Russia.

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:11.540
But the Russians weren't actually involved
in the conflict.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:16.539
And then, in 2015, it looked as if his forces
were going to be defeated.

00:20:16.539 --> 00:20:22.549
And so Putin came to the United States, he
spoke at the United Nations, he met briefly

00:20:22.549 --> 00:20:24.380
with President Obama.

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:28.960
And then, a week or two later, the Russians
started bombing in Syria.

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:33.150
The United States was involved in the Syrian
civil war, and we were supporting the forces

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:34.830
that were against President Assad.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:39.210
And so the Russians came in on the other side,
and they got involved.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.090
And Russia has stayed there.

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:47.789
And Russia and Iran together have been able
to save President Assad from being defeated.

00:20:47.789 --> 00:20:53.390
And as a result of that, Russia has also greatly
increased its footprint in the Middle East.

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:57.330
Unlike the United States, it talks to all
of the different countries there.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:58.860
It talks to Iran.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:04.230
It talks to all of the Sunni states, and it's
developed a close relationship with Israel

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.350
over the past few years.

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:11.850
So this is an example where, again, it's not
a very expensive intervention in Syria.

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:16.610
There aren't very many Russian ground troops
there, but they have their air force there.

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:18.059
They have a base there.

00:21:18.059 --> 00:21:21.930
And they've used this as a springboard to
increase their presence in a number of other

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.429
countries really quite successfully.

00:21:24.429 --> 00:21:29.700
SIERRA: Russia has a knack for never letting
a crisis go to waste and for making unlikely

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:30.700
partnerships.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:35.120
After all, there are not many countries that
can maintain fruitful relations with Iran

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.270
and Israel at the same time.

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:43.220
STENT: Many other countries in the rest of
the world, such as India, Brazil, obviously,

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:49.230
China, regard Russia as a large autocratic
country with which they can do business.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:54.450
And so Russia's relationship with the West
is very fraught: with the United States, with

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.010
the countries of Europe.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:22:01.641
But much of the rest of the world does not
have a view of Russia that is malign and thinks,

00:22:01.641 --> 00:22:03.640
again, that it's a large country with whom
they can do business.

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:08.900
And I think, sometimes in the United States,
we maybe don't understand that well enough.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:14.100
SIERRA: Still, no matter how well Russia plays
its weak hand, the point remains that that

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:15.890
hand is weak.

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:19.970
Currently, Russia is facing a number of obstacles
that will affect what the country looks like

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:23.669
in the future and its role on the global stage.

00:22:23.669 --> 00:22:29.320
STENT: One of the real challenges that Russia
faces is it has a declining population.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:31.600
It's demographically really quite challenged.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:35.990
And it's really been impossible for the Russian
government to persuade people that they should

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:37.480
have a whole lot more children.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:43.190
But also the other important thing to know
is that they have this unusually high mortality

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:44.929
rate for young men.

00:22:44.929 --> 00:22:48.640
And so that particularly affects, for instance,
the military.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:54.400
SIERRA: In 2019, the percentage of Russian
youth that said they wish to leave the country

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:56.620
reached a ten-year high.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:59.960
So what does this mean for the future of the
country?

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:02.340
And also, why would that be happening?

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:06.130
SESTANOVICH: Well, the Russian economy has
had a bad decade.

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:09.420
The first decade of Putin's rule was pretty
good.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:13.520
You had household income going up by multiples.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:20.630
And that was a big source of Putin's legitimacy
and reputation in Russian domestic politics.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:28.400
Since the global financial crisis, the Russian
economy has grown at barely 1 percent a year.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:33.320
And household income has declined several
years in a row.

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:40.510
When that happens, talented Russian young
people, particularly with a good education

00:23:40.510 --> 00:23:46.840
or foreign language skills, say, I'm out of
here, if I can find a way to get myself to

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:50.800
Western Europe, or the United States, or the
Middle East.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:53.860
They tend to look for those opportunities.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:24:02.940
As long as Russia continues in a kind of economic
decline, that sort of loss of morale will

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.330
be a national problem.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:11.299
And it's one thing that Putin and his associates
are very aware of.

00:24:11.299 --> 00:24:17.690
They keep trying to come up with plans to
turn the population decline around, to reinvigorate

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:24.320
economic growth, but so far, for most of the
past decade, they haven't succeeded.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:31.470
They have for some time thought of themselves
as, you know, a civilization in decline.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:35.029
And this too is part of the Soviet act aftershock.

00:24:35.029 --> 00:24:40.470
SIERRA: These factors signal trouble ahead
for Russia - but that trouble does not guarantee

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.650
a waning of Russia's willingness to act.

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:48.870
In fact, many observers feel that it is precisely
because of these factors - declining population,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:53.850
a troubled economy - that Russia feels it
must resort to unconventional strategies in

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:59.820
order to exert influence and to undermine
those nations that seem to be leaving it behind.

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:06.299
SIERRA: So, what do you imagine Putin might
have been feeling as he watched the chaos

00:25:06.299 --> 00:25:08.230
in Washington, DC on January 6?

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:13.289
SESTANOVICH: Well, you know, Putin has some
very mixed reactions to this.

00:25:13.289 --> 00:25:21.919
On the one hand, he has said, liberal democracy,
liberal ideas, the West's sense of superiority-

00:25:21.919 --> 00:25:23.840
we challenge all of that.

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:28.960
The conceptions that the West has had about
how to run a country are outdated.

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:36.520
On the other hand, he had hoped that Donald
Trump would be a pal, would be a kindred spirit.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:42.710
And for him to see Trump hustled out of the
office means the end of a moment in which

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:50.370
you could have had a kind of similar nationalism
that he hoped would be compatible, dominating

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:52.690
in both Russia and the United States.

00:25:52.690 --> 00:25:54.750
There's one other element of what happened.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:59.090
Putin is always unnerved by popular uprisings.

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:07.100
He really hates them because it shows there's
a kind of autonomy to the popular will.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:11.060
This isn't exactly his idea of how things
should operate.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:18.520
And he doesn't also like the idea that real
democratic constitutionalism and the rule

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:20.540
of law matter.

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:26.640
So in Washington, when the Congress was meeting
to certify a democratic election, it was doing

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:30.169
something that Putin is uncomfortable with.

00:26:30.169 --> 00:26:35.500
And when all of these thugs were storming
the Capitol and breaking windows, they were

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:38.530
also doing something that Putin was uncomfortable
with.

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:45.159
So while he likes the specter of American
weakness, he actually doesn't want people

00:26:45.159 --> 00:26:53.530
to unpack any of these events and think too
hard about how they might be exported to Russia.

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:59.409
SIERRA: Putin's fear of an uprising has been
made clear by his ruthless treatment of political

00:26:59.409 --> 00:27:00.510
opposition.

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:05.289
Take the case of Alexei Navalny, Russia's
most prominent opposition figure.

00:27:05.289 --> 00:27:10.760
In August 2020, Navalny was poisoned with
a Novichok nerve agent, a lethal and rare

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:15.240
substance that has been used to poison other
Russian opposition figures.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:21.090
He was evacuated to Germany and put in an
induced coma, narrowly surviving the attack.

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:25.340
Following our interviews for this episode,
Navalny made the extraordinary decision to

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:28.590
return to Russia, where he was promptly arrested.

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:34.669
Still, he was able to release a video message
calling for mass protests in Russia, saying,

00:27:34.669 --> 00:27:39.740
"there's nothing these thieves in their bunkers
fear more than people on the streets."

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:45.710
SIERRA: So how can the United States find
a way of creating a more stable and effective

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:47.960
relationship with Putin's Russia?

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:52.659
STENT: So I think that first of all, it's
important to know that the Russian constitution

00:27:52.659 --> 00:27:56.590
was changed this year, and Putin could now
be in power till 2036.

00:27:56.590 --> 00:28:01.210
So that will be a bit longer than Stalin was,
quite a lot longer than Stalin.

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:05.820
So the first thing we have to do is we have
to identify what are the issues on which we

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:08.990
have common interests with Russia and where
we can work with them.

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:11.190
And I think the most important one is arms
control.

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:16.390
I said at the beginning, we are the two nuclear
superpowers, we have an arms control agreement

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:22.190
that regulates our strategic nuclear weapons,
the big ones, and that expires on February

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:23.190
5, 2021.

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:28.640
And it's hoped that the new Biden administration,
and they said they want to do it, can extend

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:31.460
this agreement for five years with the Russians.

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:35.480
The Senate doesn't have to vote on it, if
not a word of the language is changed in the

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:36.480
treaty.

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:40.580
And I think that's an important area where
we have to work with Russia.

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:44.720
Now, they're developing weapon systems that
we don't like, and we're developing weapon

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:45.899
systems they don't like.

00:28:45.899 --> 00:28:50.039
But the worst thing would be if we had no
arms control agreement with them and we just

00:28:50.039 --> 00:28:51.950
have an out and out arms race.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:53.510
It's very expensive.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:55.700
It makes the world more dangerous.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:29:00.899
And it encourages other countries to acquire
nuclear weapons, what we call nuclear proliferation.

00:29:00.899 --> 00:29:06.230
DOUGHERTY: Russia, I think, affects our lives
in many different ways.

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:11.130
I don't think Russia is intent on blowing
us up, but it could.

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:15.909
It could literally destroy the United States
and the world.

00:29:15.909 --> 00:29:19.270
So we should be concerned about nuclear weapons.

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:24.730
We should be concerned about some types of
controls on nuclear weapons.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:31.300
The United States and Russia are the two countries
that have the most nuclear weapons, and they

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:34.059
are an example to the rest of the world.

00:29:34.059 --> 00:29:39.059
So that other countries, such as Pakistan,
India, and many others, look to the United

00:29:39.059 --> 00:29:44.800
States and Russia and say, "If you guys can't
come to an agreement on nuclear weapons, why

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:46.400
should we?

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:51.450
Maybe we ought to build up our weapons because
now it's a much more unstable place."

00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:57.000
STENT: Another area going forward, since the
Biden administration says it will recommit

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:02.070
itself to fighting climate change and rejoining
the Paris agreements, the Russians used to

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:04.000
be very skeptical about climate change.

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:08.720
And Putin a few years ago said there's nothing
wrong if it gets a little warmer in Russia.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.730
But recently, he's actually singing a different
tune.

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.110
He's saying he takes this very seriously.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:17.590
Russia is already suffering from some of the
impact of climate change.

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:22.840
So going forward, it's possible that that's
an area which isn't too politicized but where

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:25.409
we can work with the Russians.

00:30:25.409 --> 00:30:30.460
SESTANOVICH: Russia can be an important player
on the whole set of climate issues partly

00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:37.030
because it is greatly affected by the question
of whether or not the world makes a transition

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:40.419
to a post-carbon economy.

00:30:40.419 --> 00:30:46.149
The Russian economy is still very dependent
on oil and gas production and exports.

00:30:46.149 --> 00:30:54.220
They also are rather uniquely situated to
benefit from changes in climate that make

00:30:54.220 --> 00:31:02.470
other countries wonder whether they're as
ready to bear a lot of the burden of the transition

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:06.750
to a different economy, a different set of
post-carbon regulations.

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:10.470
STENT: There are areas where we can work with
them.

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:14.960
But the areas where it's very difficult to
work with them and where we have to be very

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:18.730
realistic are, for instance, anything to do
with their neighborhood.

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:25.000
I think you have to accept that our relationship,
at best, is one of competition and cooperation.

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:29.730
I think that the challenge now really is just
to get back to a more normal relationship.

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:34.169
In the past four years, a lot of the channels
of communication between the United States

00:31:34.169 --> 00:31:37.460
and Russia atrophied, particularly those in
the State Department.

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:39.850
I think some of those can be revived.

00:31:39.850 --> 00:31:43.240
And that at least there can be this normal
level of communication.

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:47.620
And really not to forget the Russian people
and to keep reaching out to them because the

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:50.790
government doesn't speak for all of the people.

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:54.510
SESTANOVICH: The overused quote about Russia
is that it's never as strong or as weak as

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:55.980
it seems.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:59.200
But it's always pretty strong.

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:04.340
And still, the reasons that it's strong or
also weak change.

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:08.740
If the Biden administration asks, How much
does Russia matter?

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:14.929
It will have to review all of the issues that
we've been talking about, from nuclear weapons

00:32:14.929 --> 00:32:19.909
to climate change, to imperial upheaval.

00:32:19.909 --> 00:32:25.460
But one possibility that they'll consider
is that actually, Russia matters a little

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:27.899
bit less than it used to.

00:32:27.899 --> 00:32:35.299
They may decide that a lot of these issues
where Russia matters don't actually lead to

00:32:35.299 --> 00:32:37.820
the need to cooperate with Russia.

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:44.360
One of the things that Putin probably fears
as much as anything else is being downgraded

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:52.540
in the policy calibrations of Western countries,
the idea that Russia, as much as it matters,

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:54.639
may not matter as much as it did.

00:32:54.639 --> 00:32:57.010
DOUGHERTY: It's a smart country.

00:32:57.010 --> 00:33:02.269
They have a lot of really educated young people.

00:33:02.269 --> 00:33:06.960
Many of them, I think, are patriotic, and
they want Russia to improve.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:13.620
I do think that if we can understand that
there are ways of working with even the next

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:19.380
generation, that it's important, that there
are problems that go way beyond the political

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:21.160
problems we're looking at right now.

00:33:21.160 --> 00:33:27.769
And if we look at Russia as simply an enemy,
we are going to shoot ourselves in the foot.

00:33:27.769 --> 00:33:32.270
The best way to deal with these countries,
sometimes I believe, being a former exchange

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:38.520
student in Russia myself, is to bring them
to the United States and send Americans there.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:40.060
I'll end on this.

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:42.570
Russia has a very powerful culture.

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:43.919
It always has.

00:33:43.919 --> 00:33:50.850
Their culture can enrich ours, and ours already
is enriching theirs on many different levels.

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:57.360
I think it's important for Americans to take
them seriously, and not to demonize them,

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:03.450
and not to sell ourselves short by misinterpreting
what they're doing.

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:10.000
SIERRA: In this show, we focus on serious
foreign policy issues because, well, they

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.220
matter, and they affect all of our lives.

00:34:12.220 --> 00:34:18.450
But sometimes, a nation's people can get lost
in a discussion of their government's policies.

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:23.440
The Russia we hear about in the news can seem
scary - an authoritarian country capable of

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:29.010
carrying out assassinations, annexations,
election hacks, and massive cyberattacks with

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:30.800
seeming impunity.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:36.099
It's terrifying to remember the scale of Russia's
nuclear arsenal, and our own, as treaty after

00:34:36.099 --> 00:34:38.210
treaty lapses.

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:42.690
That is the Russia that is so often used synonymously
with Putin's name.

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:49.200
But there is another Russia, the Russia of
Tolstoy, Dostoevsky, Gogol, and Chekhov.

00:34:49.200 --> 00:34:52.899
The Russia of Tchaikovsky, Rachmaninoff, and
Shostakovich.

00:34:52.899 --> 00:34:58.750
A long list of mathematicians and scientists
of towering accomplishment.

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:03.480
These are treasures that belong to the world,
and they signify Russia's place in it.

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:07.210
These, too, are reasons that Russia will always
matter.

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:13.050
So, in this spirit, we thought we'd ask our
incredible research consultant Elena Tchanikova

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:15.630
to read us out in her mother tongue.

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:17.550
See you next time, everyone!

